{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"AWS alarm sweep 2026-08-12T04:00Z → 22:45Z (read-only; CloudWatch describe/get + SSM read-only journalctl on i-00880b2c7f13527c5; nothing mutated in AWS or on the box).\n\nSCOPE. 14 metric alarms, 0 composite alarms; all 14 are OK right now. describe-alarm-history (StateUpdate) over the window returns 8 items, of which 3 are transitions INTO ALarm. The other 5 are recoveries and the two INSUFFICIENT_DATA→OK settles of the newly-created reason-scoped pair.\n\nFIRINGS.\n\n(1) rebar-autodeploy-review-interrupts [LEGACY alarm, deleted ~11:40Z] — ALARM 04:01:26Z, recovered 04:16:26Z. Driving datapoint 03:46Z bucket Sum=1. Journal marker 2026-08-12T03:59:33Z: reason=bound-exceeded, in_flight=1, deferred_for=1820s, bound=1800s, target=57d19254. TRUE POSITIVE. Boundary case: the underlying event is at 03:59:33Z, inside the greasy-plucky-piranha window that closed ~04:00Z, so this is the tail of the 4 already-triaged-and-accepted firings; only the alarm evaluation landed 86s after the baseline. Alarm behavior correct, keep as-is.\n\n(2) rebar-autodeploy-review-interrupts [LEGACY] — ALARM 08:27:26Z, recovered 08:42:26Z. Datapoint 08:12Z bucket Sum=1. Journal marker 08:26:14Z: reason=bound-exceeded, in_flight=1, deferred_for=1810s, bound=1800s, target=1791436c. TRUE POSITIVE, genuinely new since baseline. Same class as the accepted piranha findings: the drain check WORKED, DEPLOY_DEFER_MAX was spent with a review still in flight, deploy proceeded and killed it. Alarm behavior correct, keep as-is.\n\n(3) rebar-autodeploy-review-interrupts-bound-exceeded [NEW alarm, junco/1650] — ALARM 19:38:49Z, recovered 19:53:49Z. Datapoint 19:23Z bucket Sum=1. Journal marker 19:34:44Z: reason=bound-exceeded, in_flight=1, deferred_for=1930s, bound=1800s, target=f6f31dfd. TRUE POSITIVE, genuinely new. First real firing of the replacement alarm; it fired for the right reason with the right reason-scoped attribution. Alarm behavior correct, keep as-is.\n\nNo other alarm changed state in the window. rebar-autodeploy-review-interrupts-signal-unavailable never fired (it settled INSUFFICIENT_DATA→OK at 11:42:21Z and has stayed OK).\n\nNEAR-MISS / NEW DEFECT (no firing, but a fabricated metric emission).\nAt 09:48:03Z the observability probe published in ONE run: review_interrupts_bound_exceeded=7 and review_interrupts_signal_unavailable=1. Both landed in the single minute 09:48Z (period-60 query confirms). No matching events occurred then. Evidence: journal retention reaches back to 2026-07-03T19:50:40Z, so nothing rotated away; full-journal totals are bound-exceeded=8 and signal-unavailable=1; the ONLY signal-unavailable marker in the entire journal is 2026-08-04T00:36:41Z, i.e. 5.5 days older than the run that published it as 'new this interval'.\nMechanism: infra/scripts/observability.sh publish_autodeploy_marker_delta() converts a cumulative full-journal grep -c into a per-interval delta via a persisted offset file, with prev defaulting to 0 when the file is absent. The reason-scoped counters were brand new, so their first run computed new = total = all retained history. The helper already clamps the OPPOSITE direction (negative delta / lost history, per bug 2dc7-31b7-ecbb-4cd2 and the 1648 rotation double-count clamp); the cold-start case (offset ABSENT rather than journal SHRUNK) is the complementary gap and is unguarded.\nWhy it did not page: the two alarms were created at 11:40:58Z, ~2h AFTER the 09:48:03Z probe run. That ordering is luck. Under the usual terraform-then-deploy order both 1-datapoint latches would have fired instantly off stale history — including the signal-unavailable alarm, whose own description calls it 'the URGENT one ... EVERY deploy is running blind', from a single already-resolved 2026-08-04 event.\nClassified FALSE-POSITIVE-GENERATING metric defect, not an alarm-threshold problem: the alarm predicate is right to fire on a nonzero count, the count was fabricated. Filed as bug widespread-zealous-narwhal (e2a6-9ee4-8d5c-4290), open/unclaimed, file impact infra/scripts/observability.sh. Latent for every future counter added to the shared helper and for any offset-file loss (fresh /var/lib/rebar, host rebuild, disk restore); the same open-coded shape exists in sections 3 (replication_errors), 4 (voter_errors) and 4b (g2p_dispatch_errors).\n\nNOISE RATE for the piranha 'accepted, retune only if pager noise becomes a problem' disposition, so it can be revisited with data rather than quietly loosened:\n  Aug 1–11 (11 days): 6 review-interrupt firings — 08-04T00:09:45Z, 00:38:45Z, 01:45:45Z, 08-07T11:33:14Z, 12:55:14Z, 08-12T03:08:26Z ≈ 0.55/day.\n  Aug 12 alone (00:00–22:45Z): 4 firings — 03:08:26Z, 04:01:26Z, 08:27:26Z, 19:38:49Z ≈ 10x the preceding rate, matching the 60+-merge landing burst.\nAll four Aug-12 journal markers cleared the bound by a hair: deferred_for 1810s / 1820s / 1810s / 1930s against bound=1800s, i.e. 10s, 20s, 10s and 130s over. Every one of them was a review that finished (or would have) shortly after the deferral budget expired. That points at DEPLOY_DEFER_MAX being marginally undersized for current review durations, NOT at the alarm being too sensitive — a bound raise is a fix to the true-positive CAUSE, not a threshold loosening, and would plausibly have prevented all four interrupts.\n\nCORRELATION WITH KNOWN CONTEXT.\n  - JIRA_API_TOKEN expiry (until ~18:30Z, healthy run 18:34Z): produced ZERO alarm firings. rebar/host:voter_errors was 0 across the entire window, and the rebar/host namespace has no reconcile/bridge counter at all (19 metric names, none bridge-related). So bridge auth failure is currently invisible to CloudWatch. Coverage gap — recommendation only, no change made.\n  - Alarm replacement (junco/1650): confirmed. Both reason-scoped alarms carry AlarmConfigurationUpdatedTimestamp 2026-08-12T11:40:58Z and settled out of INSUFFICIENT_DATA at 11:41:49Z / 11:42:21Z. The legacy rolled-up alarm is gone from describe-alarms; its metric review_interrupts is still published (offset 9 = 8 bound + 1 signal), so pre-split history stays readable as designed.\n  - Rotation double-count clamp (1648): not implicated in any firing; it is the sibling guard to the cold-start gap that IS implicated.\n  - Heavy overnight CI/agent activity: consistent with the 10x interrupt rate; no load-metric alarm (disk, EC2 checks, bedrock, g2p, replication, mirror, merge-change) moved at all.\n\nBOTTOM LINE: 3 firings, all TRUE POSITIVES of the same known bound-exceeded behavior — no alarm should be tuned for any of them. 1 new latent false-positive defect in metric emission, filed. 2 operator decisions surfaced: whether to raise DEPLOY_DEFER_MAX, and whether to add bridge/reconcile alarm coverage."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786574909504428001,"uuid":"383a25a2-5faf-415e-9eef-0be5b15a81e1"}